ne, and fully re-initialised per CPI by `reset_cpi()`) and `sync_correction.cc`'s `static std::atomic<bool>` CUDA-failure/log-once latches (shared DEGRADATION, no data path). `test_nr_isac_python_parity` 18 cases (was 15): `test_branch_engine_routing()` (G4 test 3 in pure form — inactive/out-of-range/garbage branch ids all resolve to "drop", never to 0), `test_branch_output_identity()` (the legacy regression pin), `test_branch_engines_are_independent()` (two live engines started together, fed different CFR at different cadences with different branch tags — the first engine's closed CPI is character-identical to the same engine run alone). Both new gates proven non-vacuous by deliberate falsification. Both replay baselines UNCHANGED (34/0/24 and 34/34/31/34/34). **RESOURCE SCALING, flagged before anyone runs four branches live**: N engines means N snapshot pools, N sets of `detector.cc` thread_local CUDA/FFT workspaces and N concurrent CPI working sets — at 273 PRB the per-CPI working set is already what `CRASH_FIXES_IMPLEMENTED.md` §8 root-caused as the `std::bad_alloc`, and the binding limit there was `RLIMIT_MEMLOCK` under `mlockall(MCL_CURRENT|MCL_FUTURE)`, not free memory. Nothing in this task budgets for 4x; measure the locked-memory headroom before a four-branch 273 PRB capture. Fix round 1 (2026-09-11): two real safety defects found in review and fixed — (i) `physical_channel >= 0` and `n_active` were two DIFFERENT active-branch predicates and P03's parser allowed them to disagree (a `rx_branch_phys_map` entry for a branch not named in `rx_branches`), so `rx_branches="0"` + `rx_branch_phys_map="0:0,1:1"` built TWO engines while deriving ONE unsuffixed output identity for both — exactly the silent collision P13a exists to prevent; fixed at the ROOT in `nr_rx_branch.c`'s validation loop (the equivalence is now a parser invariant every `physical_channel >= 0` consumer already assumed) AND independently in P13a by giving both sites one `branch_is_active()` predic